eme":"open_library_subject","label":"Aliénation mentale","sourceId":"source-open-library-inconvenient-people-sarah-wise"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Droit","sourceId":"source-open-library-inconvenient-people-sarah-wise"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Conditions sociales","sourceId":"source-open-library-inconvenient-people-sarah-wise"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psykiskt sjuka","sourceId":"source-open-library-inconvenient-people-sarah-wise"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psykiatrisk tvångsvård","sourceId":"source-open-library-inconvenient-people-sarah-wise"}],"publicationYear":2012,"pageCount":480,"summary":"\"The nineteenth century saw repeated panics about sane individuals being locked away in lunatic asylums. With the rise of the 'mad-doctor' profession, English liberty seemed to be threatened by a new generation of medical men willing to incarcerate difficult family members in return for the high fees paid by an unscrupulous spouse or friend. And contrary to popular modern belief, the madwoman in the attic was at least as likely to have been a madman. Sarah Wise uncovers twelve shocking stories, some told for over a century, which reveal the darker side of the Victorian upper and middle class - their sexuality, fears of inherited madness, financial greed and fraudulence - and chillingly evoke the black motives at the heart of the phenomenon of the 'inconvenient person.' --Inside jacket.","thumbnailUrl":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/10565617-L.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"essayistic","score":1,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"essay\"","catalog text contains \"essays\"","catalog text contains \"criticism\"","literature/criticism subject or topic"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_